<!-- TELEMETRY LIMITS (2026-10-14; caps REPLACED BY REMOVAL 2026-10-15) — ClickHouse's notes about itself, OFF.

     WHAT HAPPENED (box, 2026-10-14): without this file, metric_log (server telemetry,
     NOT application data) grew to ~194k rows in hundreds of tiny parts; a single
     background merge of those parts ballooned to 6.4 GB RAM and the server burned
     60-80% of a core around the clock. On the 8 GB box that fed the kernel's OOM
     killer — which evicted the WordPress databases and took the portfolio down.
     (Owner's question then: "Is that a part of the function of the program or can we
     disable the logging?")

     WHY REMOVAL AND NOT CAPS (measured 2026-10-15, both stacks): the 100k-row caps
     written on 10-14/15 DID NOT HOLD. Box: trace_log spanned all four days since the
     stack was built — 288 million rows / 4.67 GiB — with a 100k-row cap in this very
     file since 10-14 02:01; text_log 6.9M rows; asynchronous_metric_log 80M rows.
     Local, hours after truncation: trace_log back to 1.8M rows. The merge ledger
     (part_log, last 24h) attributed the churn entirely to these tables: ~19k merges
     and ~70k part removals, ~13 GiB each way per day, ClickHouse pinned near a
     quarter of a core — on a box already swapping ~2.8 GiB. Nothing reads any of
     these tables; they are ClickHouse recording notes about itself.

     SO THEY ARE REMOVED ENTIRELY (metric_log since 10-14; trace/text/part/
     asynchronous_metric/processors_profile/opentelemetry_span since 10-15).
     KEPT: error_log and query_log (small, useful; revisit if they ever grow).
     UNTOUCHED: all of Langfuse's own data (traces, observations, scores — database
     `default`) — governance keeps working; none of it lives in these tables.

     MOUNTED BOTH SIDES — ONE FILE: the box compose
     (product/raibach-cloud-3/ids/docker-compose.yml) and the local stack
     (governance/langfuse-main/docker-compose.yml) mount this same file.

     REVERT: remove the volume line(s) for this file in the compose file(s) and
     recreate the clickhouse service. (The image's stock defaults resume — the
     unbounded behaviour that caused the incident.) -->
<clickhouse>
    <metric_log remove="1"/>
    <trace_log remove="1"/>
    <text_log remove="1"/>
    <part_log remove="1"/>
    <asynchronous_metric_log remove="1"/>
    <processors_profile_log remove="1"/>
    <opentelemetry_span_log remove="1"/>
</clickhouse>
